WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.380
Can a growth cone with the same receptor turn toward netrin in one condition and

2
00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:09.380
away in another?

3
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:13.580
Start on the map, then zoom into one growth cone in a dish.

4
00:00:14.583 --> 00:00:16.163
Receptors are one layer.

5
00:00:17.043 --> 00:00:20.183
In some cells, the growth cone's internal state is a second.

6
00:00:21.267 --> 00:00:24.467
Mu-ming Poo's laboratory developed a turning assay.

7
00:00:25.387 --> 00:00:29.927
A fine pipette puffs short, regular pulses of a cue beside one growth cone.

8
00:00:30.917 --> 00:00:34.217
Time-lapse imaging measures which way the growth cone turns.

9
00:00:35.217 --> 00:00:39.857
In 1997, Guo-li Ming and colleagues used frog spinal neurons.

10
00:00:40.737 --> 00:00:42.817
Their growth cones turned toward netrin-1.

11
00:00:43.817 --> 00:00:46.157
Next they blocked cAMP signalling.

12
00:00:47.077 --> 00:00:50.957
They blocked PKA, the enzyme cAMP switches on.

13
00:00:51.917 --> 00:00:54.537
With PKA blocked, they puffed netrin again.

14
00:00:55.537 --> 00:00:56.537
Toward, away, or no turn?

15
00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:04.370
The same growth cones turned away.

16
00:01:05.270 --> 00:01:06.770
Attraction had become repulsion.

17
00:01:10.333 --> 00:01:12.213
Now add an antibody to DCC.

18
00:01:13.093 --> 00:01:16.253
If the receptor stays the same, what should the antibody block?

19
00:01:22.317 --> 00:01:25.497
It stopped both turns, the attraction and the repulsion.

20
00:01:26.457 --> 00:01:27.477
Both needed DCC.

21
00:01:31.017 --> 00:01:32.537
The receptor was the same.

22
00:01:33.337 --> 00:01:36.797
The cell's cAMP chose the direction, in this system.

23
00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.200
This is the rule's second limit at work.

24
00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:43.440
A growth cone carrying DCC was repelled.

25
00:01:44.333 --> 00:01:46.693
It is one worked example in one system.

26
00:01:47.553 --> 00:01:50.473
It is not a rule for every netrin-responsive axon.

27
00:01:51.417 --> 00:01:53.517
Calcium can also change the response.

28
00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:58.180
So can laminin, and the eye shows why a flip might be useful.

29
00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:03.260
Retinal axons run along the inner surface of the retina to the exit.

30
00:02:04.283 --> 00:02:08.083
That exit is the optic disk, where netrin-1 is concentrated.

31
00:02:09.183 --> 00:02:14.243
In mice lacking netrin-1 or DCC, the axons still reach the optic disk.

32
00:02:15.023 --> 00:02:16.423
But they fail to leave through it.

33
00:02:17.333 --> 00:02:21.533
In frog retinal growth cones, laminin-1 lowers cAMP.

34
00:02:22.413 --> 00:02:24.753
It turns netrin's attraction into repulsion.

35
00:02:25.833 --> 00:02:28.013
Laminin lies on the retinal surface.

36
00:02:28.933 --> 00:02:32.813
In the authors' model, netrin repels where laminin and netrin overlap.

37
00:02:33.693 --> 00:02:35.973
That may help push axons down into the exit.

38
00:02:36.967 --> 00:02:41.687
The takeaway: the growth cone decides, with its receptors and, in some cells, its

39
00:02:41.687 --> 00:02:42.487
internal state.
